window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][34]={"university_physics_en:M34-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M34-L1 – Qubits, the Bloch Sphere and Projective Measurement<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M34 – Master: Quantum Information and Quantum Technologies<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12: Hilbert spaces and quantum\nstates; M33-L2: two-level systems.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Parameterize a normalized qubit\nstate and calculate its two computational-basis measurement\nprobabilities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A classical bit has one of two states. A pure qubit is instead\nspecified by normalized complex amplitudes whose squared magnitudes\nyield probabilities. The Bloch sphere represents pure states on its\nsurface and mixed states within its volume. A global phase cannot be\nobserved, whereas the relative phase between basis amplitudes can matter\nphysically.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Fixing the global phase, every pure qubit is parameterized by polar\nangle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta\\in[0,\\pi]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand azimuth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi\\in[0,2\\pi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA measurement in the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbasis follows the Born rule:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(0)=\\cos^2(\\theta/2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(1)=\\sin^2(\\theta/2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Bloch vector is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf r=(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\phi,\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi,\\cos\\theta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe density operator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝝈<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=(I+\\mathbf r\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalso accommodates mixed states with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf r|\\le1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mfrac><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mfrac><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi\\rangle=\\cos\\frac{\\theta}{2}|0\\rangle+e^{i\\phi}\\sin\\frac{\\theta}{2}|1\\rangle.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=\\pi/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe probabilities are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(0)=3/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(1)=1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Bloch vector makes a 60° angle with the positive z-axis. The figure\nshows the selected xz cross-section and the z projection. For 400\nidentically prepared ideal trials, one expects about 300 zeros, not an\nexactly prescribed count.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_234_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Bloch sphere in the xz plane with a state vector at 60 degrees and its z projection.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Bloch sphere in the xz plane with a state\nvector at 60 degrees and its z projection.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Pure qubit state and measurement axis in a simplified Bloch\nsection.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What are the probabilities at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=\\pi/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What Bloch z component follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(0)=3/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the Bloch surface not represent every mixed state?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(0)=P(1)=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_z=P(0)-P(1)=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Mixed states generally have a shorter Bloch vector in the sphere’s\ninterior.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The sphere represents one two-level system and does not replace the\ntensor product for multiple qubits. A measurement axis and basis must be\nspecified. Frequencies fluctuate even for perfect state preparation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Qubit amplitudes and phases underpin entanglement and gate\noperations. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M34&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/basics-of-quantum-information\">IBM Quantum Learning – Basics of Quantum Information<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Qubit states, measurements, Bell states and elementary circuits; topics and selected formulas; Subject relevance to lesson: M34-L1 – Qubits, the Bloch Sphere and Projective Measurement · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/quantum-channels/quantum-channel-basics\">IBM Quantum Learning – Quantum Channel Basics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Density matrices and quantum channels; the noise-mixing parameter is expressly defined within this lesson; Subject relevance to lesson: M34-L1 – Qubits, the Bloch Sphere and Projective Measurement · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M34-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M34-L2 – Entanglement, Bell States and CHSH Correlations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M34 – Master: Quantum Information and Quantum Technologies<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12: tensor products and spin;\nM34-L1: qubits.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish the local CHSH bound\nfrom the quantum prediction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>An entangled two-qubit state cannot be written as a product of\nindividual state vectors. In the Bell state |Φ⁺⟩, computational-basis\noutcomes are perfectly correlated even though each marginal outcome is\nrandom. Correlation by itself does not prove entanglement: appropriately\nchosen Bell tests reveal the difference.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For the spin singlet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ψ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>01<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\Psi^-\\rangle=(|01\\rangle-|10\\rangle)/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncoplanar spin measurements have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(a,b)=-\\cos(a-b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDefine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=E(a,b)+E(a,b\\prime)+E(a\\prime,b)-E(a\\prime,b\\prime)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA locally realistic model subject to the Bell-test assumptions gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|S|\\le2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nquantum theory allows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis does not imply faster-than-light signaling.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|S|_{\\mathrm{LHV}}\\le2,\\qquad |S|_{\\mathrm{QM}}\\le2\\sqrt2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\prime=\\pi/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=\\pi/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\prime=-\\pi/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe correlators in order are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1/\\sqrt2,-1/\\sqrt2,-1/\\sqrt2,+1/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2.828<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=-2\\sqrt2\\approx-2.828<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure compares the absolute local bound with the quantum maximum\nand is not experimental data.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_082_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Comparison of absolute CHSH bounds: local value two and quantum maximum about 2.83.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison of absolute CHSH bounds: local\nvalue two and quantum maximum about 2.83.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Absolute CHSH bounds for local models and ideal quantum\ncorrelations.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the maximum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|S|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a local CHSH model?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What value follows for the chosen angles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can one not directly send a bit using the singlet?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.828<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\sqrt2\\approx2.828<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Local outcomes are random; correlations appear only after classical\ncomparison.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The CHSH bound assumes the relevant Bell-test conditions. Detection\nefficiency, random setting choices and loopholes matter experimentally;\nthe statement is not about arbitrary statistical correlations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Entanglement is a resource for quantum-information protocols. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M34&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/multiple-systems/quantum-information\">IBM Quantum Learning – Quantum Information and Bell States<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Representation and interpretation of entangled Bell states; Subject relevance to lesson: M34-L2 – Entanglement, Bell States and CHSH Correlations · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/tutorials/chsh-inequality\">IBM Quantum Documentation – CHSH Inequality<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · CHSH correlators and experimental violation; measurement angles in the lesson are an original example; Subject relevance to lesson: M34-L2 – Entanglement, Bell States and CHSH Correlations · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M34-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M34-L3 – Quantum Gates, Circuits and Bell-State Preparation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M34 – Master: Quantum Information and Quantum Technologies<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M34-L1: qubit amplitudes; M34-L2:\nBell states.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive a Bell state from |00⟩\nusing H and CNOT and calculate measurement correlations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Quantum circuits separate reversible unitary operations from\nmeasurements. A Hadamard gate H creates a superposition from |0⟩. A\ncontrolled-NOT (CNOT) flips its target when the control is in basis\nstate |1⟩. Together the two operations transform an initially separable\nstate into an entangled one.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Start in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>00<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|00\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nApplying\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo>⊗<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(H\\otimes I)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>00<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(|00\\rangle+|10\\rangle)/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe subsequent\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{CNOT}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconverts the second branch into\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|11\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nComputational-basis outcomes are 00 and 11, each with probability 1/2.\nThat distribution alone does not distinguish coherent entanglement from\na classical mixture; other measurement bases are needed.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo>⊗<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>00<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>00<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{CNOT}(H\\otimes I)|00\\rangle=\\frac{|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>The figure shows two input wires, a Hadamard gate on the upper wire\nand a subsequent CNOT controlled by that wire. For 200 ideal\nmeasurements, approximately 100 outcomes 00 and 100 outcomes 11 would be\nexpected. Outcomes 01 and 10 do not occur ideally.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_231_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two qubit wires: a Hadamard on the first qubit followed by CNOT from the first to the second.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two qubit wires: a Hadamard on the first\nqubit followed by CNOT from the first to the second.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Circuit preparing the entangled state Φ⁺.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the state immediately after H acts on the first qubit?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the ideal probability of 01 at the circuit output?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How can one distinguish the coherent state from a classical 00/11\nmixture?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>00<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(|00\\rangle+|10\\rangle)/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By using correlations measured in an additional suitable basis, such\nas the X basis.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The circuit idealizes perfect qubits and gates. Real hardware has\npreparation, gate and readout errors. Gates may be physically\nimplemented through different pulse sequences or interactions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Circuits underpin quantum algorithms and error diagnostics. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M34&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/basics-of-quantum-information/quantum-circuits/circuits\">IBM Quantum Learning – Quantum Circuits<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Hadamard/CNOT circuits preparing a Bell state; Subject relevance to lesson: M34-L3 – Quantum Gates, Circuits and Bell-State Preparation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/basics-of-quantum-information\">IBM Quantum Learning – Basics of Quantum Information<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Qubit states, measurements, Bell states and elementary circuits; topics and selected formulas; Subject relevance to lesson: M34-L3 – Quantum Gates, Circuits and Bell-State Preparation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M34-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M34-L4 – Grover Search and Quantum Amplitude Amplification<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M34 – Master: Quantum Information and Quantum Technologies<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M34-L3: quantum gates; M31: numerical\nsimulation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compute the ideal success\nprobability of Grover search for a single marked item.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Unstructured search among N candidates requires many classical checks\nin the worst case. Grover’s quantum algorithm uses an oracle phase mark\nand inversion about the mean (diffusion) to amplify target amplitudes.\nIts advantage concerns ideal oracle queries and does not automatically\ninclude state preparation, data loading or hardware overhead.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequally marked items among\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npossibilities, define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta=\\sqrt{M/N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomplete Grover iterations, the target probability is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>r<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_r=\\sin^2((2r+1)\\theta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn appropriate integer iteration count is roughly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><mi>π<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r\\approx\\frac\\pi4\\sqrt{N/M}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>≪<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\ll N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nexcessive iterations lower success again. This rotation assumes an ideal\noracle and diffuser.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>r<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arcsin<\/mi><msqrt><mfrac><mi>M<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_r=\\sin^2\\!\\left((2r+1)\\arcsin\\sqrt{\\frac{M}{N}}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arcsin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=\\arcsin(1/2)=\\pi/6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nInitially\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_0=1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nafter one iteration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_1=\\sin^2(\\pi/2)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter two iterations it falls back to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe plotted ideal curve illustrates periodic amplitude rotation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_121_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Grover success probability for four items and one marked solution over zero to three iterations.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Grover success probability for four items\nand one marked solution over zero to three iterations.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Amplitude amplification peaks after one iteration.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=1,N=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many complete Grover iterations are ideal here?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(\\sqrt N)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot imply a guaranteed total machine runtime?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">One iteration.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Oracle implementation, state preparation, gate noise and sampling\nadd overhead.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The formula does not provide a quadratic speedup for arbitrary\nproblems. The number of solutions M must be known or estimated; real\nmachines impose noise and finite-precision costs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Algorithmic advantages are incomplete without a model of noise. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M34&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://learning.quantum.ibm.com/course/fundamentals-of-quantum-algorithms/grovers-algorithm\">IBM Quantum Learning – Grover’s Algorithm<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Amplitude amplification and query complexity of unstructured search; Subject relevance to lesson: M34-L4 – Grover Search and Quantum Amplitude Amplification · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M34-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M34-L5 – Density Matrices, Decoherence and Quantum Channels<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M34 – Master: Quantum Information and Quantum Technologies<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M34-L1: Bloch sphere; M34-L3: ideal\ngates.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate the output state and\nmeasurement probabilities for an explicitly defined mixing channel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Real qubits interact with their surroundings and control electronics.\nPure vectors are insufficient when experiments involve mixtures or lost\ncoherence. A density operator is Hermitian, positive semidefinite and\nhas unit trace. A physical quantum channel is completely positive and\ntrace preserving (CPTP), so it acts consistently even on entangled\ninputs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>As a teaching example take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal E_p(\\rho)=(1-p)\\rho+pI/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le p\\le1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt mixes the input with the maximally mixed qubit and is not the only\nparameterization of a Pauli-error channel. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho_0=|0\\rangle\\langle0|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe output is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{diag}(1-p/2,p/2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEvery Bloch vector shrinks by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nreducing purity and measurement contrast.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℰ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>I<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal E_p(\\rho)=(1-p)\\rho+\\frac p2I,\\qquad 0\\le p\\le1.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Start with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|0\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(0)=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(1)=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the Bloch-vector length is 0.8. The figure compares the initial and\nshrunken z components and labels the measurement probabilities. The\nparameter value is illustrative, not a hardware benchmark.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_068_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Ideal Bloch vector shrinks to 80 percent under a mixing channel; outcome probabilities are labeled.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Ideal Bloch vector shrinks to 80 percent\nunder a mixing channel; outcome probabilities are labeled.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Illustrative isotropic mixing channel applied to one input\nqubit.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|0\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the Bloch length at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is positivity alone insufficient for arbitrary entangled\ninputs?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthe state is maximally mixed.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The channel extended with an untouched partner must still map\ndensity operators to positive operators.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The mixing channel does not represent every decoherence mechanism.\nDephasing and amplitude damping affect states differently; one parameter\nis not a complete hardware characterization.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Quantum channels make noise modelable and motivate error correction.\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M34&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/general-formulation-of-quantum-information/quantum-channels/quantum-channel-basics\">IBM Quantum Learning – Quantum Channel Basics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Density matrices and quantum channels; the noise-mixing parameter is expressly defined within this lesson; Subject relevance to lesson: M34-L5 – Density Matrices, Decoherence and Quantum Channels · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M34-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M34-L6 – Quantum Error Correction with the Three-Qubit Code<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M34 – Master: Quantum Information and Quantum Technologies<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M34-L3: CNOT; M34-L5: quantum\nchannels.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the syndrome table for\none X error and the resulting logical failure probability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>An unknown quantum state cannot be cloned arbitrarily. Quantum error\ncorrection instead encodes quantum information coherently across a\nlarger Hilbert space. The three-qubit repetition code maps α|0⟩+β|1⟩ to\nα|000⟩+β|111⟩. Parity checks can reveal which physical qubit suffered a\nbit-flip X error without reading the logical amplitudes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>The stabilizers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_1Z_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_2Z_3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave outcomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(+1,+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the error-free codespace. Errors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nproduce\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1,+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1,-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(+1,-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrespectively. If every physical qubit flips independently with\nprobability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nmajority decoding fails for two or three flips. Hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{\\mathrm{fail}}=3p^2(1-p)+p^3=3p^2-2p^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{\\mathrm{fail}}=3p^2(1-p)+p^3=3p^2-2p^3.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na physical bit-flip occurs with probability 10%, while ideal encoding\nand single-round correction yield\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.028<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{\\mathrm{fail}}=0.028=2.8\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram maps four syndromes to no error, X1, X2 and X3. Additional\nnoisy gates and syndrome measurements are deliberately excluded from\nthis calculation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_241_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four Z-parity syndromes mapped to one-qubit bit flips or no error.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four Z-parity syndromes mapped to\none-qubit bit flips or no error.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Syndrome mapping for the ideal three-qubit bit-flip\ncode.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which syndrome identifies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What logical failure fraction follows for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does the repetition code by itself correct arbitrary Z phase\nerrors?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1,-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.104<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3(0.2)^2-2(0.2)^3=0.104<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Additional encoding/stabilizers are necessary for phase-error\ncorrection.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The estimate assumes independent bit-flip-only noise and perfect\nsyndrome extraction. Correlated errors, leakage, phase flips and faulty\ncorrection circuits can erase the advantage.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Fault tolerance connects abstract codes with reliable hardware. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M34&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://learning.quantum.ibm.com/course/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors\">IBM Quantum Learning – Correcting Quantum Errors<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Three-qubit repetition code and distinct bit-/phase-flip errors; Subject relevance to lesson: M34-L6 – Quantum Error Correction with the Three-Qubit Code · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M34-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M34-L7 – Quantum Sensing, Measurement Precision and Quantum Networks<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M34 – Master: Quantum Information and Quantum Technologies<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M32: instrumentation; M33: atomic and\noptical physics; M34-L2: entanglement.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain the ideal 1/sqrt(N)\nprecision scaling for independent probes and distinguish it from network\nprotocols.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Quantum technologies use controlled states for sensing and\ninformation transfer. Atomic clocks and interferometric sensors convert\nan unknown phase into statistical counts. Entanglement or squeezing can\nimprove precision under suitable assumptions, but loss, dead time and\ntechnical drift preclude a universal gain. Quantum networks distribute\nstates or entanglement and still need classical control and\ncommunication channels.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nideal independent probes at optimal operating point and fixed contrast,\ntypical phase uncertainty scales as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\phi\\propto1/\\sqrt N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is shot-noise scaling; its prefactor depends on the actual sensor.\nCorrelations can permit a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nscaling under specific ideal resource counts, but are susceptible to\nnoise. Precision scaling says nothing by itself about security of\nquantum key distribution.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>∝<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\phi_{\\mathrm{independent}}\\propto\\frac1{\\sqrt N}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Increasing the count of independent ideal probes from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>400<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=400<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhalves phase uncertainty: in a normalized example from 0.10 to 0.05 rad.\nThe graph shows theoretical values for several N and is not a\ncharacterization of a particular clock or sensor. Networks can\ncoordinate measurements but do not improve the scaling without extra\nresources or a suitable protocol.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_232_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Ideal phase uncertainty decreases with probe count as one over square root N, highlighting N 100 and 400.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Ideal phase uncertainty decreases with\nprobe count as one over square root N, highlighting N 100 and\n400.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Normalized shot-noise scaling for ideal independent\nprobes.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By which factor does ideal uncertainty change when N is\nquadrupled?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does ideal variance scale if standard deviation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\propto N^{-1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a quantum measurement bound not a network-security\nguarantee?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Uncertainty is halved.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">As\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Security depends on protocol assumptions, implementation,\nauthentication and side channels.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The formula applies to independently measured ideal probes, not every\nsensor. Technical noise can impose a floor. Quantum networks require\nlow-loss components and validated protocols; topical references are no\nsubstitute for security evaluation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>This module links single-qubit physics to correlations, circuits,\nalgorithms, noise, error correction and practical applications.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/quantum-information-science/quantum-sensing-explained\">NIST – Quantum Sensing Explained<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Quantum sensors, atomic clocks and measurement applications; does not verify every precision-limit formula; Subject relevance to lesson: M34-L7 – Quantum Sensing, Measurement Precision and Quantum Networks · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/publications/optimal-quantum-measurements-expectation-values-observables\">NIST – Optimal Quantum Measurements of Expectation Values of Observables<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Ideal 1/sqrt(N) and 1/N scaling under different measurement assumptions; Subject relevance to lesson: M34-L7 – Quantum Sensing, Measurement Precision and Quantum Networks · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/programs-projects/quantum-communications-and-networks\">NIST – Quantum Communications and Networks<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Development and characterization of quantum networks; no unconditional security guarantee; Subject relevance to lesson: M34-L7 – Quantum Sensing, Measurement Precision and Quantum Networks · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
